findings.claim:
The design plans for the 'same-named rule is not replaced' case and says to extend the attempt-1 entry 'into a single entry, never a second copy'. But it renamed the rule, so the engine has already appended it as a second entry under a new name, beside attempt 1's. The design does not say which entry survives. If the build keeps only the new four patterns, Read-Mark's own form with the `c` dropped (`$WatchedTags -contains $_[0]`, :207) matches none of them:
- `\btag\s+` needs the word 'tag';
- `\$[\w.]*tag\b` stops at `$_[0]`;
- the `$_[0]` pattern needs `$_[0]` on the left.
Attempt 1's first pattern catches it.

findings.fix:
In (d), state:
